The theme for the first week of Advent is hope. What is hope? What are we hoping for? What is our hope based on?

When doing a quick search of the word *hope* in the Bible, it appears at least 130 times in the Old and New Testaments. A quick look at many of these references shows us that hope is always in the Lord. When we look at some verses where faith and hope are mentioned, it seems that hope is in the Lord and faith is the action of that hope.

We hope in a lot of things and sometimes we use the words hope and faith interchangeably. Sometimes their lines dance together because one cannot go without the other when it boils down to it. Both faith and hope might look foolish in the eyes of the rest of the world. Only someone whose hope is rooted in God can continue to hope in the face of results that would seem to prove otherwise.

For example, we have been hoping for another child in our family for a few years now, with each passing month making it seem more impossible. I was hoping that Alpha would really take hold in our church and launch off into the greater Rock Hill area. So far, both have proven my hope to seem "futile and ridiculous" by the standards of our world. But perhaps my hope in these things was misplaced. Maybe my energy and attention had become too focused on the outcome; another child, a successful ministry.

Over the past few years I have come to realize that when my focus is on the outcome rather than the Giver, my theology started getting wonky. Why suffer and grieve through secondary infertility? Why did He call me to this ministry if He wasn't going to prove it successful? Do you see how these hopes can slowly turn into idols? My heart and mind started to get so focused on the grief and complaints that it was getting hard to see the beauty of my God, and dare I say it, I even found myself questioning my faith.

Is it wrong to hope for those things? Not at all.

God has shown Himself to me in deeper and more meaningful ways through this season of grief. There has been such beauty in learning to praise and give glory to God through suffering. There is a richer and deeper love He has instilled in me, and I am thankful for it. I am finally starting to see that even if these hopes do not come to the conclusion I am hoping for, I still have faith in a God who is good. He does not need to fulfill those hopes because my greater hope is in Him, and He proves faithful.

Faith seems to be hope in action. My faith comes from a faithful God. My hope is in Him.

Which brings me to this week's Alpha question: **How Can I Have Faith?**

If I'm being honest, this is not merely a question for those who are exploring Christianity for the first time. It is a question I have found myself asking in seasons of waiting, disappointment, and uncertainty. When the things we hope for seem far away, how do we continue to trust God? How do we keep believing when we cannot yet see what He is doing?

Advent invites us into that very tension. We wait, we hope, and we trust that Christ is near even when the fulfillment of God's promises seems distant.

Nicky Gumbel, the developer of the Alpha series,&nbsp; says, "'Faith' and 'being sure' are not opposed." The writer of Hebrews says, "Now faith is being sure of what we hope for and certain of what we do not see (Hebrews 11:1)" St. Augustine wrote, "God does not expect us to submit our faith to Him without reason, but the very limits of our reason make faith a necessity. Faith is the first step to understanding. Understanding is the reward of faith. Therefore, seek not to understand that you may believe, but believe that you may understand."

The Gospel reading in our Advent service liturgy echoes this same reality. Jesus says:

"Now learn the parable from the fig tree: as soon as its branch becomes tender and sprouts its leaves, you know that summer is near. So you too, when you see these things happening, recognize that He is near, right at the door." (Mark 13:28-29)

The fig tree gives signs that something is coming before it fully arrives. In many ways, that is where we find ourselves during Advent. We are still waiting, still hoping, and still trusting in the promises of God. We do not yet see the fullness of His Kingdom, but we trust that He is near.

This passage also brings to mind the painting *The Light of the World* by William Holman Hunt, which is discussed in this week's Alpha episode. The painting was inspired by Jesus' words in John 8:12:

"I am the light of the world. Whoever follows me will not walk in darkness, but will have the light of life.” and under the frame in St. Paul’s Cathedral is writing from Revelation 3:20 “Behold, I stand at the door and knock. If anyone hears my voice and opens the door, I will come in to him and eat with him, and he with me.”

As I continue to learn, hope is not ultimately found in getting the things I want, even when those desires are good and God-given. My hope is in the One who remains faithful whether those hopes are fulfilled or not. This Advent, may we learn to wait, trust, and recognize that He is near, right at the door.

**Application**&nbsp;

Take some time this week to study the picture and discuss with your small group the little things you notice. What time do you think it is? Why isn’t there a handle? Where do you see yourself in this picture?&nbsp;

